  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nThe mid-level ridge will be drifting and lifting northeastward \nbecoming centered over the St. Louis area by Tuesday. The broad \ntrough in the western US will persist keeping our low level flow \nfrom the southwest and the storm track passing over northern \nportions of the state with a surface quasi-stationary boundary in \nplace in that vicinity as well. The sensible weather effects will be \ndrier and warmer conditions over southern Iowa with opportunities \nfor storm chances over northern Iowa with a timing peaking likely \novernight into the early morning hours. This is not a forecast to be \nvery specific with fine tuning of the details such as timing and \nlocation in the coming days. What seems more confident is the late-\nseason heat with potential for several days of record warm low \ntemperatures possibly in jeopardy, pending any convective \ndevelopment farther south in Iowa and cloud debris contamination. \nWhile heat index values never breach the 105 degree advisory \ncriteria, there will be multiple days of values peaking in the 100-\n103 degree range over southern Iowa paired with low temperatures in \nthe upper 70s through at least Wednesday. Experimental heat risk is \nin the extreme category (level 4 of max of 4) over many portions of \nsouthern Iowa during this period, which aligns with the magnitude of \nthe uncommon late season heat and high low temperatures. Those with \nvulnerabilities to heat will want to take precautions through this \nupcoming week.",
